From 5d283474b56022af039fc0d020f914e695095ac0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher A. Williamson <contact@chrisaw.io>
Subject: [PATCH] netd: make the oneshot self-test non-fatal on legacy kernels

Source: cawilliamson/treble_restlessos, android-17.0
Upstream commit: 5d283474b56022af039fc0d020f914e695095ac0
License/provenance: RestlessOS repository declares its patch/build system Apache-2.0;
the original author and source are retained here for attribution.

On GSI devices with legacy vendor kernels, the one-shot BPF initialization can
fail even though the real netd process has a non-fatal fallback. Returning that
failure from the pre-start check can trigger reboot_on_failure before zygote.

---
 server/main.cpp | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/server/main.cpp b/server/main.cpp
index e84899e4..9fb09f28 100644
--- a/server/main.cpp
+++ b/server/main.cpp
@@ -123,7 +123,11 @@ bool initDnsResolver() {
 int main(const int argc, const char* argv[]) {
     if ((argc == 2) && !strcmp(argv[1], "oneshot")) {
         setenv("NETD_ONESHOT", "1", 1);
-        return libnetd_updatable_init("/sys/fs/cgroup");
+        // GSI: the oneshot self-test result is not decisive on legacy kernels:
+        // libnetd_updatable_init may fail without eBPF, while the real netd
+        // process handles the failure through its normal startup path.
+        libnetd_updatable_init("/sys/fs/cgroup");
+        return 0;
     }
 
     Stopwatch s;
